messages:
rachel: Working on the Next starter UI: using the SDK streaming endpoint to render tokens as they arrive. Seeing duplicated tokens and occasional UI jumps when using the LangChain streaming adapter. Anyone seen streaming + React suspense weirdness?

marc: Hmm, what stream API are you using? Are you consuming the ReadableStream in the client or passing server-sent events from the app route?

rachel: Client-side fetch to /api/generate which proxies to Redwood SDK stream. Code simplified: 

```js
// client-side
const res = await fetch('/api/generate');
const reader = res.body.getReader();
while(true){
  const { value, done } = await reader.read();
  if(done) break;
  const chunk = new TextDecoder().decode(value);
  setTokens(prev => prev + chunk);
}
```

rachel: With LangChain's callback handler attached to the SDK LLM wrapper, callbacks fire for partial tokens and then again when the final chunk merges, so UI shows repeated segments. Also when network blips happen the stream reconnects and replays recent tokens.

oliver: I hit similar duplication when streaming from server components + client suspense boundaries. The root cause was the SDK's stream wrapper retrying on small 5xxs and replaying last N bytes because it didn't track already-consumed offsets. Filed an issue: https://github.com/redwood-inference/sdk/issues/912

infra-bot: CI alert: examples/next-streaming-starter integration tests failed on commit 7bf2a1 (streaming.spec.ts flakey). Logs: https://ci.redwood.com/builds/streaming-4567

marc: Quick workaround: de-dupe at the UI level by keeping a short rolling buffer and ignoring immediate repeats (simple hash of last 64 chars). Ugly but reduces visible duplication.

rachel: Not ideal. Users expect smooth streaming for chat UX. Is the SDK able to expose chunk offsets or dedupe tokens at the client-level in a supported way?

sdk-team: Investigation notes: 1) current HTTP client does automatic retry for idempotent endpoints but doesn't know about partial-stream semantics. 2) LangChain callback adapter emits both partial and final chunks, which is expected. Plan: add a stream-safe retry mode that includes monotonic byte offsets and Idempotency-Key metadata so server can resume without replaying already-delivered bytes.

ux-team: From product perspective, we should ship a streaming best-practices doc in the starter showing how to integrate LangChain callbacks with a token-accumulator and how to handle reconnects. Also include an example that uses SSE (EventSource) to simplify the client logic.

oliver: Proposed short-term patch: 1) SDK: disable automatic retries for streaming endpoints unless `streamRetryMode: "resume"` is explicitly set. 2) Add `x-resume-offset` header when enabled. 3) Examples: add client helper `useStreamingRedwood` that de-dupes and exposes `onPartial`/`onFinal`. I'll draft PR.

rachel: Can you add a tiny example in the starter that uses EventSource? That was way simpler for our students. Example snippet: 

```js
// /app/components/stream-client.js
useEffect(() => {
  const es = new EventSource('/api/sse-generate');
  es.onmessage = (e) => setTokens(t => t + e.data);
  es.onerror = () => es.close();
  return () => es.close();
}, []);
```

marc: We'll also add a test in examples/next-streaming-starter that simulates a mid-stream 502 and verifies resume behavior when streamRetryMode="resume".

infra-bot: Ticket created: DEVEX-315 - Streaming resume & de-dupe for Next starter.

rachel: Thanks team. Landing the SDK change + example will make the starter much better for chat demos. Will update the starter README with the de-dupe pattern until resume mode ships.

sdk-team: ETA for PR: ~4 business days. We'll include changelog entry and examples update. Ping us if you want to pair on the EventSource sample.
